@page "/docs/get-started/cli"

@* The izzy command (src/IzzyBlazor.Cli). Its help text and exit codes are in Cli.cs; keep the two in step. *@

<ArticlePage Section="get-started" Slug="cli">
    <Lead>
        The <Code>izzy</Code> command makes new apps, sets up IzzyBlazor in the app you have, and adds
        <Anchor Href="blocks">blocks</Anchor> and pages to it as code you own. Every change can be shown first, as a diff,
        or as JSON for agents and scripts.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Install</Heading>
        <Code Block Language="bash">dotnet tool install -g IzzyBlazor.Cli --prerelease</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The CLI's version is the library's. To update it, run the same command with <Code>update</Code> in place of
            <Code>install</Code>.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Commands" Caption="Commands" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Command" Width="Length.Rem(14)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">Add a block</Heading>
        <TextBlock>Run <Code>izzy add</Code> in the app's folder, with the block's settings as options:</TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add hero --layout Split</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            The block's component goes in <Code>Components/Blocks</Code>, and the block goes on its page: a section at the end
            of <Code>Home.razor</Code>, a footer in the layout. <Code>--into</Code> names another page, and
            <Code>--at</Code> puts it on a line, as an editor's cursor does. A recipe, such as an empty state, goes only where
            you put it:
        </TextBlock>
        <Code Block Language="bash">izzy add empty-state --at Components/Pages/Orders.razor:24</Code>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>izzy describe hero</Code> lists a block's settings, and each block's page on this site shows them beside
            its preview. The settings have the same names as the starter's options in <Code>dotnet new</Code>. What izzy
            writes is yours: change the words, the layout and the code.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            In an app whose pages run in the browser, blocks go in the <Code>.Client</Code> project with the pages. The
            parts that read the request, such as the cookie notice's, stay in the server project.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">See the changes first</Heading>
        <Table Items="Previews" Caption="Showing changes" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Option" Width="Length.Rem(14)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="What it does">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            <Code>--json</Code> prints the files to create, the text to insert (with its offset and a hash of the file it
            was read from), the packages, the warnings and the files that clash. An agent can show it for review, then run
            the same command without <Code>--dry-run</Code>.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Files you've changed</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            izzy never replaces a file without <Code>--overwrite</Code>. When a file it would write is there with other
            content, it writes nothing and names the file; compare the two with <Code>--diff</Code>. A line it adds to one of
            your files, such as a <Code>@@using</Code>, is skipped when it's there already.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Each block added is recorded in <Code>.izzy/items.json</Code>, with its version and settings. Commit it with the
            app.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">Exit codes</Heading>
        <Table Items="ExitCodes" Caption="Exit codes" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Code" Width="Length.Rem(5)"><code>@context!.Name</code></TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Meaning">@context!.Text</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">Next steps</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="blocks">Blocks</Anchor>: every block, with its settings and a live preview.</ListItem>
            <ListItem><Anchor Href="docs/get-started/ai-assistants">AI assistants</Anchor>: IzzyBlazor's docs server for your coding agent.</ListItem>
        </List>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Text);

    private static readonly Row[] Commands =
    [
        new("izzy new <starter> -n <name>", "Makes a new app, as dotnet new does. See Create an app."),
        new("izzy init", "Sets up IzzyBlazor in the app you have. See Add to an existing app."),
        new("izzy add <item>", "Adds a block or page to the app."),
        new("izzy list", "Lists the catalogue: starters, blocks, pages and recipes. --kind block lists one kind."),
        new("izzy describe <item>", "An item's settings. With --json, also the components it uses, its support in each render mode and its accessibility notes."),
    ];

    private static readonly Row[] Previews =
    [
        new("--dry-run", "Writes nothing, and lists what would change."),
        new("--diff", "Shows the changes as a unified diff."),
        new("--json", "Prints the changes as data. list and describe take it too."),
    ];

    private static readonly Row[] ExitCodes =
    [
        new("0", "Done, or nothing to change."),
        new("1", "An error, such as an unknown setting, or an app that doesn't build after izzy init."),
        new("2", "Files there with other content: nothing was written."),
        new("3", "A Pro item or layout. Pro layouts are listed and marked; they arrive with IzzyBlazor Pro."),
    ];
}
